    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d305">

                <pb facs="0365" n="1199" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 657.119/979</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Schmedeman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Christiania</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-10-24T19:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 24, 1918, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11.37 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1277. From Owen to War
                    Trade Board:</p>
                <p>No. 168. Draft of corkmakers’ benevolent [<hi rendition="#italic">branch</hi>]
                    agreement approved by you in Department’s No. 436, August 24, 5 p.m., No. 52 for
                        Dye, is not in
                    accordance with instructions in Department’s 396, July 26, 4 p.m. Other branch
                    arrangements are being drawn up in the same manner as corkmakers’ agreement.
                    Should not the words “War Trade Board” be substituted for “United States
                    Government” in first paragraph of these draft agreements, and should the term
                    Associated Governments be employed through these forms?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schmedeman</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
